WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:02.430
- Today I want to talk
about three principles

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.100
that changed my life and these learnings

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.040
are influenced by my six
years working in consulting

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.130
at McKinsey, but also my subsequent roles

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:14.850
in industry and startup
as a C-level executive.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.343
So welcome to another coffee break here

00:00:17.343 --> 00:00:18.562
on my channel, Firm Learning.

00:00:18.562 --> 00:00:19.740
I'm sticking to water
today, because it is already

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.450
quite late here in Germany.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.280
And without further ado, I want to start

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.770
with the first principle,
and this is about

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:28.920
how to achieve financial freedom,

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.230
but I must warn you, because the truth,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.660
what I'm going to share,
is maybe quite different

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.121
from what you are expecting.

00:00:36.121 --> 00:00:38.299
Many people seem to believe that saving

00:00:38.299 --> 00:00:40.170
is the best way to
achieve financial freedom,

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.050
and I also myself have
talked about the importance

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.250
of saving in the past.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.770
I will link some videos
about this above there.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.080
But what does financial
freedom really mean?

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.120
And surely there are different
definitions out there.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.490
What I like is the definition that is used

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:54.990
by the FIRE movement.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:59.190
FIRE stands for financial
independence, retire early.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.641
It's a group of people
set themselves the goal

00:01:01.641 --> 00:01:04.590
to not work throughout
their whole working life,

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.860
but at some point stop
working and be able to live

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.740
just from your capital
and the capital gains,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.150
and this is exactly the definition

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.650
I want to adopt for this video.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.020
Financial independence
means that you don't need

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.458
to work anymore and you can live

00:01:18.458 --> 00:01:19.530
purely on your capital gains.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.030
But what would it really take

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.830
to achieve financial independence?

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.740
And one rule commonly applied
in the personal finance

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.150
community is the 4% rule, and
this means that on average,

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.250
if you have your capital invested in a mix

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.460
of stocks and bonds, you can
expect that you can achieve

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.080
a 4% pre-tax return in
perpetuity, more or less,

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.090
without bankrupting your capital.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.270
And from this 4%, you deduct about 25%

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.883
as capital gains tax
as is commonly applied,

00:01:48.735 --> 00:01:50.532
for instance, in Germany
and other countries,

00:01:50.532 --> 00:01:54.000
you will end up with
about 3% post-tax returns

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.770
that you can expect on your capital.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.600
This is, of course, a
simplified calculation,

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.010
but one that might be quite helpful here.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:02.790
So if you work with such a
3% rule, what is the capital

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.310
that you need in order to
be financially independent?

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.470
Of course, the number
differs for everyone.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:11.370
In my simple mind, I'd say
if you have four million euro

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.380
or $4 million, doesn't matter that much

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.210
in the current euro-dollar parity.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:19.800
If you have four million euro
invested and apply 3% to that,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.890
that means that you have
actually a net disposable income

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.570
after tax of 120,000 euro per year,

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.120
or 10,000 euro per month.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.510
So 10,000 euro per month
for your free disposal.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.640
I'd say most people would say that yes,

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.280
they would consider themselves
financially independent

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.630
if they achieved this.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.776
With this rule of thumb of 3%,

00:02:41.776 --> 00:02:44.130
you can calculate for yourself
what your target number

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.320
would be, but let's, for
the sake of this argument,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.660
stick with the four million euro.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:49.830
So what do you need for that?

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.500
What does it take to
have four million euros

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.763
saved at some time?

00:02:54.613 --> 00:02:55.830
Well, let's imagine there is someone

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:00.830
who earns on average 100,000
thousand euros per year gross

00:03:01.847 --> 00:03:03.480
before tax, based on annual employment,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.350
on average throughout their
working life of 40 years.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.390
And arguably, if you were able to do that,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:12.554
you are surely one of the
more successful people, right?

00:03:12.554 --> 00:03:14.640
I'd argue it's quite difficult on average

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.100
over a period of 40 years to earn

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.280
such a high-profile
salary, even if you work

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:23.430
in a high-paced careers
in banking and so on, why?

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.736
Because of course, the first
years you will earn less,

00:03:26.736 --> 00:03:27.569
because you're just a junior.

00:03:27.569 --> 00:03:29.160
Then maybe there's a
certain period in your life

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.540
where you have these high salaries,

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.400
but then many people
also during the course

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.064
of their career at some point
also start earning less again,

00:03:36.064 --> 00:03:37.320
because maybe also these fast-paced

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.630
types of jobs are not right for them.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:40.560
This is my perspective.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.450
Let me know what you think
about this in the comments.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.090
But if you stick with
these numbers, so 40 years,

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.080
100,000 euro per year, this
means that after 40 years,

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:52.170
you will have earned
four million Euro, right?

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:55.140
So these are more or less
your lifetime earnings

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.520
for someone working in a
fast-paced, high-profile career.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.110
So based on this, you can see that yes,

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.470
it is possible to have four
million euros lifetime earnings,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.290
but the problem, of course,
is that this is before tax,

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.240
before social security contributions.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.640
This can easily consume
half of this amount,

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.500
and then you need to pay for rent,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.230
for living expenses, for
the tuition of your kids.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.140
So even if you are such a high earner,

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:23.460
saving four million euro
will be extremely difficult

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.890
if you follow a common career path,

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.990
even in one of these high-profile careers

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.360
I talk so often about on this channel.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.220
And this is exactly the first key insight,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.560
the first implication I
want to share with you

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.450
in this video, and this
is something that took me

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.300
quite a lot of time to figure
this out, but this means

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.440
that if you want to truly
achieve financial independence,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.770
it is very, very, very
difficult to do this

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.860
based on a regular employment,
based on a regular job.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:54.120
Again, even if you do work
for such a high-paced career,

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.090
of course, there are some
positions where this is possible.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.130
Of course, there are
some people who achieve

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:02.160
maybe in considering a
partner, a senior partner rank,

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.110
and are able to keep such a role

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:05.910
throughout their professional life.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.700
Of course, these people
will be able to achieve

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.010
these types of financial
guards, but of course,

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.710
these are only a very
small minority of people,

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.300
and I think you would not be wise

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.790
to follow strategy that
this would be exactly

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:21.014
the type of position that
you will find yourself in

00:05:21.014 --> 00:05:22.023
at some point.

00:05:22.023 --> 00:05:22.856
So what is the learning from that?

00:05:22.856 --> 00:05:25.500
Well, if at some point you
do want to improve your life

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.590
to the better, you do want
to achieve financial freedom,

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.600
following this definition,
which, of course,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.970
might not be the goal
of everyone, of course,

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:34.560
maybe you might have another vision,

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.540
that is completely fine for you as well.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.620
You need to do to some extent
something out of the ordinary.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.930
You need to look into entrepreneurship,

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.150
into developing your side projects,

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.820
doing something next to your regular job,

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.780
otherwise, just mathematically,
it's almost impossible

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:52.740
to achieve this.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:56.250
The next principle I got a lot
out of for my personal life

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.850
is about avoiding the tyranny of the or,

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:02.220
and this is from a book I
picked up as a teenager.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.090
It's from Jim Collins'
bestseller "Built to Last,"

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:07.530
a book I can very much recommend.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.910
There's a link to it in
the video description.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.410
Feel free to pick it up yourself.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.650
And one of the things that
Jim Collins describes here

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:17.400
is that successful people are
able to avoid exactly that,

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.050
the tyranny of the or.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.840
The tyranny of the or
describes a belief system

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.830
that many people think that
things can either be A or B,

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:27.180
but not the same time.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.610
It can either be like this or like that,

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.530
but having both things at the same time,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.300
doing both things at the same time,

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.540
both things being true at the
same time is not possible.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.240
This is a contradiction that
many people have in their head,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.640
and there are lots of examples for that.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.260
I can either be a good person

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.570
or I can climb up the corporate ladder.

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:48.150
I can either spend time with my family

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:51.660
or I can use the evenings
to go out for sports.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.990
I can either have fun
during my time in university

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:58.380
or I can be a good student
and write A's all the time.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.720
Either or, or, or, or, or, or.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.967
This exactly is the tyranny of the ors,

00:07:03.967 --> 00:07:06.150
this limiting belief that
you cannot have it all,

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.310
that you cannot make
through several things

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:09.420
at the same time.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.400
And how Jim Collins describes it,

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.670
the successful people are
able to actually embrace

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:16.950
the genius of the and.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.590
It is very often absolutely possible

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:22.230
to achieve many of these,
maybe on the surface,

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.090
contradictory goals at the same time.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.550
I claim that all of the
things I just talked about,

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.610
probably it is possible to
achieve all of them together,

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:33.030
and this is exactly a very
important mindset to adopt.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:35.220
To some extent, maybe you
could claim that also my life,

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:36.690
I adopted exactly this mindset

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:38.430
by starting the YouTube channel, right?

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:40.446
I get questions all the time.

00:07:40.446 --> 00:07:42.330
How is it possible to
work a full-time career

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.400
and also do all these YouTube videos?

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.110
Well, I do think that it is possible.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.330
It's about priorities,
it's about wanting it,

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:49.950
setting yourself your goals.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.510
So ask this for yourself.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:53.460
What is your YouTube channel?

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:55.510
Of course, not claiming that everyone

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:56.549
should start a YouTube channel,

00:07:56.549 --> 00:07:57.660
but what does this mean for you?

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:01.800
Where can you embrace the
genius of the and to enrich

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.400
your life, both personally
and professionally?

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.490
The next principle I'd like
to call being in the right

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.950
versus winning in life.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:13.230
And my grandmother once
asked me, Heinrich,

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:14.970
do you want to be in the right

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:17.910
or do you want to achieve your goats?

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.310
Some people are obsessed
with being in the right.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:23.490
They're literally willing
to lose all their existence,

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.650
to lose their sympathy that
other people have for them,

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:29.700
to lose money just to
prove other people wrong.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.760
This is such a common
theme of human behavior

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:35.130
that the German author Heinrich Von Kleist

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:36.870
wrote a whole novel about it.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:41.580
Michael Kohlhass, this tragic
actor who lost his whole life,

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.340
his whole existence indeed, just to prove

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:45.960
that he was in the right.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.880
It's a classic of German literature.

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.490
I very much recommend you
to pick this up as well.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:52.950
Examples for this behavior, big and small,

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.410
can be witnessed almost every day.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.510
People willing to lose their sympathy

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:00.810
just to prove someone wrong,
people suing each other

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:03.720
over trivial stuff,
people getting involved

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:06.990
in heated arguments about
things that don't matter at all,

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:09.210
people correcting each other over things

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:11.430
that just do not matter for sure.

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:13.140
And yes, while I do not want to claim

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:16.410
that this is always wrong,
sure, there's some situations

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:18.870
where such a behavior would be justified,

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.570
I would claim that very
often this type of behavior

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:26.280
is usually just motivated by
fragile ego and wounded pride.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.170
Of course, there's the natural instinct

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.510
to do something against it.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.670
Always take a step back, ask yourself

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.500
what you actually want to achieve

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.230
and whether this really
helps you to get there.

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:39.902
So I'd love to Now at your view on this.

00:09:39.902 --> 00:09:40.735
Do you agree with these principles?

00:09:40.735 --> 00:09:42.420
Anything you'd like to add, I trust

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.270
this will be very insightful
for other people watching this.

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:47.520
If you took any value
out of this video at all,

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:48.930
please destroy the like button

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:51.030
for the YouTube algorithm, subscribe.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.220
Big, big thanks to the
members of the channel.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.943
My name is Heinrich.

00:09:54.943 --> 00:09:55.860
I release regular videos
here on the channel.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.723
See you again next week and bye-bye.

